  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 224 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nA slowly building area of large scale ridging will lead to \npersistent upper-level northwest flow into the Midwest region \nthroughout the remainder of the work week. Dry conditions are \nexpected to keep hold, with warming temperatures Tuesday as \nincreasing 850mb temps move into the state, especially west around \n18-20C, and increasing winds from a tightened pressure gradient and \nefficient daytime mixing, leading to highs in the mid to upper 70s \nacross Central Iowa. An area of Canadian high pressure sinks down \ninto the region once again by midweek, with temperatures a touch \ncooler in the low to mid 70s forecast as winds shift \nnorth/northwesterly. By Thursday into Friday, a weak shortwave \ndescending the eastern fringe of the aforementioned ridge looks to \nmake its way into Iowa, with a return of mid-level moisture and \nforcing that will introduce the next potential for rain/storm \nactivity. The GFS is showing coverage over the southwest portion\nof the state Thursday evening into Friday, with dry conditions \nelsewhere given the lingering area of high pressure centered \nover the Ohio Valley. The Euro however has a direct west to east\npassage of this activity across Central Iowa Thursday afternoon\nto evening. Certainly notable differences between long range \nmodels, but consensus on rain and storms returning somewhere \nnonetheless, so will have to monitor trends over the next \nseveral days to better pinpoint coverage and timing details. \nLate week into the weekend shows a building ridge over the \ncentral US, with temperatures warming overhead as highs in the \nmid to upper 70s are expected, along with a return of dry \nconditions once again, but also notably breezy.",
